---
// Method sheet — Ducking (2026-11-01). The six parts of the method-sheet duty (werkgruppe spec §3.5).
import MethodSheet from '@/components/experiments/MethodSheet.astro'
import { readDesk } from '@/lib/ducking/desk-data'
import { FLOOR_DB, fmtCount, WINDOW_DAYS } from '@/lib/ducking/mix'

const REPO = 'https://github.com/frankbueltge/frankbueltge.de'
const link = 'underline decoration-line underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-fg'
const { round, ledger, sources, whole, book } = readDesk()
const sample = book.rest.sample
const record = book.record
---

<MethodSheet title="Method sheet — Ducking" workHref="/ducking" workLabel="To the work">
  <section class="sheet-reveal mb-10">
    <h2 class="kicker mb-2">What this is</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed">
      A share of attention, set as a mix. The fields of the ecology’s map and everything else on Common Ground’s
      charts are channels on one desk; each channel’s level is its share of the trailing week, and each is a voice. The claim is
      checkable from the committed files: how little of what the charts held named any of these fields. The desk adds no finding
      to that count. It makes the count something you have to hold up.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">1. Sources &amp; licences</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <li>
        The map and the briefing — the fields and where they stand are the ecology’s
        <a class={link} href="https://github.com/frankbueltge/research-ecology/blob/main/briefings/MAP.md">map</a>, what presses in them
        is <a class={link} href={round.briefing.url}>{round.briefing.title}</a>, written {round.briefing.written}, pinned to its commit.
        The figures the voices read were checked one by one against their sources, last on {round.checked}, and were corrected where
        the source said otherwise. Each has the page it comes from.
      </li>
      <li>
        Common Ground’s day files, {ledger.days[0]} to {ledger.days.at(-1)} — open data (CC0) in
        <a class={link} href={`${REPO}/tree/main/src/data/trending`}>src/data/trending</a>, {sources} sources on the newest day.
      </li>
      <li>
        The voices — Kokoro-82M, open weights under Apache 2.0, run locally on one machine, at no cost and through no service.
        The recordings are CC BY 4.0 with the rest of the lab’s works.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">2. Cadence</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      The faders move with Common Ground’s ledger: the build after each day’s ledger counts the new day, and the page shows the
      week to the newest committed day. The voices do not. A round is recorded once, when a checked briefing arrives, by
      <a class={link} href={`${REPO}/blob/main/scripts/ducking/voices.py`}>scripts/ducking/voices.py</a>, so its readings stay at
      the briefing’s date until the next round says otherwise, and the page gives that date.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">3. Processing</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <li>
        <b class="text-fg">Count.</b> A signal is one entry on one chart on one day. It names a field when its label or a link title
        contains one of the field’s terms as a whole word, case and accents folded, by the rule of
        <a class={link} href="/werke/third-reading">Third Reading</a>. The terms are fixed in
        <a class={link} href={`${REPO}/blob/main/src/lib/briefing/map.ts`}>src/lib/briefing/map.ts</a>. A signal naming two fields counts half for each, so the
        channels and everything else always add up to the day’s signals. This differs from the briefing’s own count, which counted
        raw occurrences in the files; a signal here is a chart entry, not a mention.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b class="text-fg">Share.</b> A channel’s signals over all signals in the trailing {WINDOW_DAYS} days. The first days of the
        record have shorter windows. The crowd is the remainder, so the sum is the whole ledger.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b class="text-fg">Level.</b> The share as a power ratio: ten times its logarithm, in decibels. The fader’s travel runs from
        0 dB, the whole, to −{Math.abs(FLOOR_DB)} dB, stretched at the top as on any desk, and each printed mark is ten times the one below. A channel at zero rests on the floor. Each
        channel’s amplitude is the square root of its share, and every recording is levelled to the same speech loudness, so a
        fader’s position means the same on every channel.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b class="text-fg">Holding.</b> A held channel takes the share its fader says. Everything not held takes what is left, in its
        measured proportions; a channel at zero gets nothing from the room that opens. Hands asking for more than the whole are
        scaled down to it. Holding changes the sound for the person holding, and no count. Nothing done at the desk is sent or kept.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b class="text-fg">The crowd.</b> Several voices read a seeded random sample of the labels of the week the round ends,
        each label once. Of {fmtCount(sample.labels)} distinct labels that name no field, {fmtCount(sample.long)} are left out as too
        long to read in passing and {fmtCount(sample.unreadable)} because they carry a non-Latin script or an emoji the voices cannot
        say, so the crowd is more Latin-script than the charts are. Left out as well, because a voice would spell them out sign by
        sign: web addresses, code and formulas, {fmtCount(sample.stopped)} this round. Nothing is left out for what it says: since
        2026-11-08 the crowd reads the charts’ words as they stand, deaths, crimes, sex and swearing among them. The rule is in the
        script. {fmtCount(sample.spoken)} labels are spoken; the sample is drawn once per round, and it loops.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b class="text-fg">The record.</b> One recording of the {Math.round((Date.parse(record.to) - Date.parse(record.from)) / 86400000) + 1} days from {record.from} to {record.to}, played at {record.day} seconds
        a day. Of {fmtCount(record.sample.signals)} signals that named a field in those days, {fmtCount(record.sample.readable)} distinct headlines can be
        read aloud: the label itself names the field, it has at least three words, it is not too long, and it holds nothing the
        voices cannot say, by the crowd’s rule, so the record is more Latin-script than the ledger. Nothing is left out for what it
        says. App stores, code hosts and coin charts are left out, because they name things and say nothing. {fmtCount(record.sample.spoken)} headlines are spoken. Each field gets a number of them in proportion to
        its signals in those days, at least one if it has any, spread evenly along its own signals, so that a dense week stays dense.
        Each is read once at the same level by its field’s voice, on about the day it was counted, later if that voice is still speaking.
        So on the record a field is heard by how often it speaks, not by how loud. The crowd is held at a seventh of its amplitude behind the closed filter while it plays. The faders go on showing
        the measured shares. Days after {record.to} have no words until the next round.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b class="text-fg">What is added.</b> Two things. A small synthetic room, in which a quiet channel stands further away than a
        loud one; it changes distance, not level. And a lowpass filter on the crowd, which closes as a hand raises a field, so that a
        voice can be understood before it has taken most of the mix. That one does change what you hear: a fader shows the level going
        into the filter, and the blurred crowd is quieter than its fader says. Measured on 2026-11-01 by transcribing three readings
        against the crowd: with the crowd open, a voice at 10 % of the mix could not be made out at all and took about 80 % to be
        recovered fully; through the closed filter it was recovered at 10 %. There is no other sound.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">4. Limits of the method</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <li>The denominator is the tops of {sources} public charts and feeds: encyclopaedia pages, search trends, app stores, code hosts, social and news sources, each with its own fixed length. It leans to technology and to English, and a longer chart weighs more. Its shares are shares of those charts, not of the world’s attention, however the desk feels.</li>
      <li>A chart measures what rose, was clicked or was posted, by whoever those platforms reach. It says nothing about what anyone thinks or cares about, and nothing about the people a field’s harm happens to.</li>
      <li>A signal is counted the same whatever its size: the top of an encyclopaedia’s list and the bottom of a feed weigh one each.</li>
      <li>The terms are a choice, wide on purpose, in English only. They let in false friends, which the receipts on the work’s page show, and they miss a field discussed in other words or other languages. The receipts cannot show what was missed.</li>
      <li>AI’s fader counts a headline only when it names AI and a harm together (since 2026-11-02): a harm put in other words is missed, and so is one that names AI only by a product’s name. Until that day every mention of AI counted, and the fader stood above all others together, largely because developer platforms and app stores talk about AI all day.</li>
      <li>The fields are one map’s, drawn from what serious bodies measure: thirteen since 2026-11-02, eleven before. They are not a ranking, and the desk does not treat them as one. One signal can name two fields and then counts half in each.</li>
      <li>The readings are short and they stop at the day they were checked. Most figures in them are themselves estimates, and where a primary source could not be used a report on it is linked.</li>
      <li>A desk invites the idea that the mix could be set otherwise by someone at a desk. Nobody sets this one. The metaphor is the work’s, not a finding.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">5. Compute footprint</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      The count runs inside the site’s build, over {fmtCount(whole.total)} signals to date, and costs no service. The voices were
      rendered in a few minutes on one desktop machine, once per round. All audio plays in the visitor’s browser, and loads only
      after the visitor asks for sound.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">6. Change log</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <li>2026-11-08, night — nothing is left out for what it says. Until now a stop list kept a label or a headline off the crowd and the record when it held a word for sex, killing, death, crime or accidents, a swear word, a web address or a dollar sign, on the reasoning that a machine reading those aloud would make a statement the ledger does not. In the round after cycle 012 it kept 118 of 774 readable headlines off the record, 88 of them war and violence and 14 that named a sum in dollars, and 133 labels off the crowd; matching fragments of words, it also silenced “programming”, “skill”, “scraped” and “Essex”. The crowd and the record now read the charts’ words as they stand. Only a web address, code or a formula, which a voice would spell out sign by sign, and a script the voices cannot say stay out. Both were rendered again; the fields’ own readings did not change.</li>
      <li>2026-11-02, evening — AI’s fader counts AI and its harms. A headline counts only if it names AI and a harm together: work lost, risk and loss of control, misuse, lawsuits and bans, the bubble, energy and water, people, slop. Until now every mention of AI counted, and most of the field was app-store entries, product news and developer posts, which the record read aloud. About one in seven of those signals names a harm. Levels before and after this day do not compare; the crowd and the record were rendered again.</li>
      <li>2026-11-02, later — the map grows to thirteen fields: nature, air and water leaves the climate field, debt, work and the cost of living joins, war takes in the violence beyond it. The round after cycle 011 took the two new faders the same evening rather than waiting for the next briefing: their lines were not in that briefing, and were written from the map’s sources, each figure read at the publisher’s own page. The monitored-wildlife matter moved from climate to nature, war took a line on homicide and inequality one on femicide, and the whole round was rendered again, at a lower bitrate (voices 40 kbps, the crowd and the record 64), so that thirteen channels still stay under the six megabytes a phone is asked to load. A voice now belongs to its field, not to its place on the desk, so every field keeps the voice it had.</li>
      <li>2026-11-02 — the channels are now the eleven fields of the ecology’s map instead of the ten matters of one briefing, so that a fader means the same from round to round and the record can be read across time. Each voice reads where its field stands, then the briefing’s matters in it. The play button became the record: real headlines, read by field, with the crowd held back. The first version’s lines below stay as they were.</li>
      <li>2026-11-01, later — the ledger under the faders changes: from the day file of 2026-11-02 Google News is read in ten English-language editions instead of the American one alone, because news was about three per cent of the signals. The record shows a step on that day, and shares from before and after it are not the same measure. Committed days are not rewritten.</li>
      <li>2026-11-01, the same day — a check of the briefing against its sources found 27 of 69 claims wrong, out of date, overstated or not on the linked page. The readings were corrected and recorded again, among them the state of the Strait of Hormuz, which a voice had stated as of early September.</li>
      <li>2026-11-01 — first version, round {round.id}. Over the 61 days at launch the ten matters held 0.99 % of the ledger. This line stays as it is; the work’s page goes on counting.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</MethodSheet>
